---
/**
 * hospitality / AboutBK — "THE VILLA" (Villa Marindu / BK).
 *
 * ROLE: the section that describes the property itself. The design draws a two
 * column split — the story on the left (eyebrow, Prata headline with its italic
 * brass emphasis, the owner's paragraphs, then a two-up hairline SPEC LEDGER of
 * label/value facts) and a single tall photograph on the right.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every number below is lifted verbatim from
 * the ABOUT element of "Villa Marindu Landing.dc.html":
 *   grid            minmax(0,1.05fr) / minmax(0,.95fr), gap 64px, centred
 *   headline        margin .26em 0 .36em over the wrapper's .h2 clamp
 *   paragraphs      16.5px / 1.85 / --grey / 52ch  → the wrapper's .lede
 *   ledger          repeat(2, minmax(0,1fr)), gap 0 34px, 30px above it
 *   ledger row      the wrapper's .rowrule (flex, baseline, 12px, 12px 0,
 *                   1px --rule-soft) — label 14.5px --bone, value 13px --muted
 *                   pushed right with margin-left:auto
 *   photo           560px tall, --r-lg corner, --well ground
 * Nothing here is re-derived to survive a colour scheme and no column has been
 * moved to suit an editor mechanism. The hex the design writes maps 1:1 onto the
 * wrapper's tokens (#B99552 --accent · #F4F1EA --bone-hi · #A9AFA9 --grey ·
 * #EFEAE0 --bone · #767C76 --muted · #212926 --rule-soft · #1A211E --well ·
 * 3px --r-lg), so this file contains no literal hex (R1).
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every one declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts,
 * so each data-field below is a hook a real sidebar input owns):
 *   about.tag              — eyebrow (.kick)
 *   about.headline         — h2, set:html so an owner's own <em> lands wherever
 *                            they put it in their own sentence
 *   about.lead             — the FIRST paragraph. Rendered here on purpose: the
 *                            sidebar's "Lead paragraph" box writes about.lead,
 *                            and a schema field no component draws is the mirror
 *                            of a dead hook and just as broken.
 *   about.paragraphs.${i}  — the remaining paragraphs (set:html)
 *   about.specs.${i}.label — the ledger's left cell
 *   about.specs.${i}.value — the ledger's right cell
 *   about.image            (data-image-field) — the photo column
 * AboutBK is the FIRST consumer of about.specs; the field was added to the
 * schema for exactly this ledger.
 *
 * R2 — NOTHING VISIBLE IS SUPPLIED BY THIS FILE. Not one word. The design's demo
 * copy ("The villa", "Built for the sea, not for a brochure.", the six sample
 * spec rows) is the DESIGNER filling their own template, and the image-slot's
 * "Drop the pool or terrace" is an editor affordance, not page copy — none of it
 * ships. A villa that wrote no spec rows advertises none.
 * Every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag, so an empty value removes the ELEMENT: no
 * empty eyebrow, no headline over nothing, no empty plate, and — because a
 * .rowrule IS its own hairline — no rule drawn under a spec row that has neither
 * a label nor a value.
 *
 * DERIVED (computed here, never invented):
 *   · paraRows / specRows drop blank entries but carry the ORIGINAL array index
 *     into data-field (R6), so deleting row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3.
 *   · a spec row survives on EITHER cell — a label with no value yet is still a
 *     line the owner typed; only a wholly empty row is dropped.
 *   · the grid drops to ONE track whenever only one of the two columns has
 *     content, so a missing photo never leaves a 560px hole beside the copy and
 *     copy that has been cleared never leaves one beside the photo. Above 900px
 *     the surviving column then keeps the MEASURE the design gives it — 638px of
 *     copy, 577px of photo, which are literally the design's two tracks at a
 *     1280px wrap — rather than stretching across the page. This is the common
 *     case, not an edge one: derive-content-defaults seeds no about.image, so a
 *     copy-only ABOUT is what a first build renders, and a ledger free to span
 *     1280px would set each label and its value at opposite ends of the page
 *     instead of drawing the design's ruled ledger.
 *   · about.specs / about.paragraphs are read tolerantly (array, string, or
 *     objects vs bare strings). Draft content has arrived as both shapes before
 *     and a `.map is not a function` takes the whole page down, not one row.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5): tag and headline are deliberately NOT counted.
 * lib/derive-content-defaults.ts sets about.tag = 'About' on every site and
 * about.headline = `About ${name}` on every named one, so a gate that counted
 * them would be always-true and would ship a heading over an empty section. The
 * gate counts BODY content only: the lead, the surviving paragraphs, the
 * surviving spec rows, the photo.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED, so none of these reads as an oversight:
 *   · about.signature and about.role as VISIBLE text. They belong to a host
 *     identity row, which is the BJ design; the ABOUT element here draws a spec
 *     ledger and a photograph and no person. about.signature is still READ, once,
 *     as the photo's accessible name — see below.
 *   · a .cap pill on the plate. The design's about photo carries no caption, and
 *     there is no schema field that would let an owner write one.
 *   · any button or link. The design's ABOUT has no action, so this file has no
 *     href hook — and therefore no copy of the onPage() anchor-remap helper the
 *     sibling sections carry. There is nothing here to remap; a helper that is
 *     never called is dead code. The anchor duty this section DOES own is its
 *     id="about", one of PageBK's page anchors and the target the header nav and
 *     the `about`/`host`/`why` aliases resolve to.
 *   · band-panel. The design draws ABOUT on the plain --night ground.
 * Vertical rhythm is the wrapper's: PageBK's shared `section { padding:
 * clamp(60px,8vw,100px) 0 }` owns it. The design's asymmetric `100px 34px 0` is
 * a wrapper-level rhythm decision, not something one section overrides on its own.
 *
 * R9 — the plate renders ONLY when there is a real upload, so role="img" is
 * honest by construction. Its accessible name is about.signature, the owner's
 * OWN words (derive-content-defaults seeds it with the business name, so on a
 * villa the photo announces as the villa). Never a literal written here. With no
 * signature the photo is HIDDEN from the accessibility tree rather than
 * announced as something we guessed, via a single conditional aria-hidden —
 * never a literal one sitting beside it.
 *
 * R3 — one scoped <style>, no is:global. The italic-brass <em> rule lives in
 * PageBK as a global on purpose: Astro's scoped styles cannot reach elements
 * created by set:html, so an emphasis rule written here would never apply to the
 * owner's own markup. Anything this file ever needs to say about an owner <em>
 * has to go through :global().
 *
 * R4 — no buttons in this section, and none of the engine-pinned class names
 * (.btn-primary .btn-ghost .nav-cta .cta-band .trust .testi, `.hero` on a
 * <section>) appear anywhere below.
 */
// layout is accepted but unused: PageBK passes it to every section uniformly,
// and this block reads nothing outside content.*.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;
const about = content.about || {};

const tag = about.tag || '';
const headline = about.headline || '';
const lead = about.lead || '';
const image = about.image || '';

// Read tolerantly: a draft has arrived with this as a bare string before, and a
// `.map is not a function` costs the whole page rather than one paragraph. The
// only fallback taken is the SAME path as a string — the BJ sibling also folds
// in a legacy `about.body`, which is not done here because the data-field would
// then point at about.paragraphs.0 while the text lives somewhere else, and an
// input that edits a different string than the one under it is a broken hook.
const paragraphSource: any[] = Array.isArray(about.paragraphs)
  ? about.paragraphs
  : (typeof about.paragraphs === 'string' && about.paragraphs.trim()
    ? [about.paragraphs]
    : []);
// A blank row in the admin's paragraph list would otherwise render an empty <p>
// that still spends its 1em margin. Drop the blanks, carry the ORIGINAL index.
const paraRows = paragraphSource
  .map((text: any, i: number) => ({ text: String(text ?? ''), i }))
  .filter((row) => row.text.trim().length > 0);

// Same discipline for the ledger. An item may legitimately be an object
// {label,value} (what the schema's newItem creates) or, from older drafts, a
// bare string — treated as a label with no value rather than dropped.
const specSource: any[] = Array.isArray(about.specs) ? about.specs : [];
const specRows = specSource
  .map((row: any, i: number) => {
    const item = row && typeof row === 'object' ? row : { label: row, value: '' };
    return {
      label: String(item.label ?? '').trim(),
      value: String(item.value ?? '').trim(),
      i,
    };
  })
  // EITHER cell keeps the row: a label the owner typed but has not priced yet is
  // still their line. Only a wholly empty row goes, so no hairline is drawn under
  // a row that is not there.
  .filter((row) => row.label.length > 0 || row.value.length > 0);

// The photo's accessible name — the owner's own words, never a literal.
const photoName = String(about.signature || '').trim();

// Track count. Computed in the frontmatter so the published grid never keeps an
// empty 560px column beside a single populated one.
const hasCopy = Boolean(tag || headline || lead) || paraRows.length > 0 || specRows.length > 0;
const hasPhoto = Boolean(image);
// R5 — BODY content only. tag and headline are excluded: both are derived onto
// every site, so counting them would make this gate always-true.
const hasSection = Boolean(lead || image) || paraRows.length > 0 || specRows.length > 0;
---
{hasSection && (
  <section class="villa" id="about">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class:list={['villa-grid', !(hasCopy && hasPhoto) && 'villa-grid--solo']}>
        {hasCopy && (
          <div class="villa-copy">
            {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="about.tag">{tag}</span>}
            {headline && <h2 class="h2 villa-h reveal" data-field="about.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
            {/* about.lead comes from a plain textarea, so it is bound as TEXT.
                The paragraph LIST below is the place an owner writes markup. */}
            {lead && <p class="lede villa-p reveal" data-delay="1" data-field="about.lead">{lead}</p>}
            {paraRows.map((row, k: number) => (
              <p
                class="lede villa-p reveal"
                data-delay={String((k % 3) + 1)}
                data-field={`about.paragraphs.${row.i}`}
                set:html={row.text}
              ></p>
            ))}
            {specRows.length > 0 && (
              <div class="villa-specs reveal" data-delay="2">
                {specRows.map((row) => (
                  /* .rowrule is the wrapper's shared label/value hairline — the
                     design's spec row exactly. Each cell is guarded on its own so
                     a half-filled row prints one cell, not one cell and a gap. */
                  <div class="rowrule villa-spec">
                    {row.label && (
                      <span class="villa-spec-l" data-field={`about.specs.${row.i}.label`}>{row.label}</span>
                    )}
                    {row.value && (
                      <span class="villa-spec-v" data-field={`about.specs.${row.i}.value`}>{row.value}</span>
                    )}
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
            )}
          </div>
        )}
        {hasPhoto && (
          <div class="plate villa-plate reveal" data-delay="1">
            <div
              class="ph"
              data-image-field="about.image"
              role={photoName ? 'img' : undefined}
              aria-label={photoName || undefined}
              aria-hidden={photoName ? undefined : 'true'}
              style={`background-image:url('${image}');`}
            ></div>
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* THE DESIGN'S SPLIT, verbatim: 1.05fr of copy against .95fr of photograph,
     64px apart, the two columns centred on each other. --solo collapses to one
     track when only one column has content, so nothing is ever left beside a
     hole. */
  .villa-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.05fr) minmax(0, .95fr);
    gap: 64px;
    align-items: center;
  }
  .villa-grid--solo { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .villa-copy { min-width: 0; }
  /* The design has no one-column ABOUT, so --solo is a state this file has to
     answer for. The family answer is to keep the surviving column at the MEASURE
     it has in the design rather than let it stretch across the whole wrap
     (HeroBJ's `.a-grid--solo .a-h1`, AboutBI's `.nofig` / `.noprose` caps).
     It matters most here: a copy-only ABOUT is the DEFAULT first build —
     derive-content-defaults seeds no about.image — and a ledger free to span
     1280px prints a label and its value at opposite ends of the page, which is
     not the ruled ledger the design draws. 638px and 577px ARE the design's two
     columns at a 1280px wrap (1216px of track split 1.05fr / .95fr), so this
     restores the design's proportions rather than inventing new ones. Below
     900px the single track is everybody's layout and the caps lift. */
  @media (min-width: 901px) {
    .villa-grid--solo .villa-copy { max-width: 638px; }
    .villa-grid--solo .villa-plate { max-width: 577px; }
  }

  /* The eyebrow is the wrapper's .kick (10px caps, .44em, --accent) — the
     design's span, already named. Restating it here would fork it from the rest
     of the template. The one thing ADDED is a wrap rule: the eyebrow is the only
     string in this section the design leaves unprotected, and .kick is an
     inline-block carrying .44em of tracking — one long owner word (a hyphenless
     place name, "HOUSEKEEPING") would push the document sideways at 360px (R8).
     Adding properties the wrapper never sets, so nothing is forked. */
  .villa-copy .kick { max-width: 100%; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* The headline is the wrapper's .h2 (Prata 400, clamp(31px,4vw,54px)/1.1,
     --bone-hi); only the design's own margins are set here. Every rule is
     qualified with .villa-copy so it out-specifies the wrapper's :where()
     primitives whatever order the stylesheets land in. */
  .villa-copy .villa-h { margin: .26em 0 .36em; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* The design's paragraph IS the wrapper's .lede — 16.5px / 1.85 / --grey /
     52ch — so only the rhythm is set here. PageBK's `:where(p){margin:0 0 1em}`
     is (0,1,1) and beats a bare .lede, hence the parent-class qualifier. */
  .villa-copy .villa-p { margin: 0 0 1em; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  /* The design gives its last paragraph a 30px gap before the ledger; that gap
     is spent by .villa-specs below, so the last paragraph gives up its own
     margin and a section with no ledger ends flush. */
  .villa-copy .villa-p:last-of-type { margin-bottom: 0; }
  /* A cleared headline is a real case, and then the eyebrow would sit flush on
     the first paragraph — the h2's .26em top margin is what normally opens that
     gap. Adjacency, so it fires only in that case, and qualified because the
     shorthand above would otherwise re-zero the top margin. */
  .villa-copy .kick + .villa-p { margin-top: 14px; }

  /* THE SPEC LEDGER — two equal columns, 34px apart, no row gap: the rows carry
     their own 12px padding and hairline (the wrapper's .rowrule), which is what
     makes a continuous ruled ledger rather than a stack of separated cards. */
  .villa-specs {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 0 34px;
    margin-top: 30px;
  }
  /* .rowrule supplies flex / baseline / 12px gap / 12px 0 padding / the 1px
     --rule-soft underline / min-width:0. Only the two cells are typed here. */
  .villa-copy .villa-spec-l {
    font-size: 14.5px; color: var(--bone);
    min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* `text-align: right` is the ONE property here the design does not write, and
     it is a no-op in the state the design shows: margin-left:auto already puts a
     single-line value hard against the right edge. It only takes effect when a
     value WRAPS — at 520px, or on a long owner value — where the design's own
     flex row would leave the second line ragged-left under a right-aligned
     first. Keeping the block right-aligned is the design's alignment continued
     into a state it never had to draw, not a new one. */
  .villa-copy .villa-spec-v {
    margin-left: auto; text-align: right;
    font-size: 13px; color: var(--muted);
    min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* The photo column. .plate already gives the --well ground, the --r-lg corner
     and the overflow clip; the design's fixed 560px height is the one thing it
     does not know. */
  .villa-plate { height: 560px; }

  /* ── One column before the copy measure is squeezed. At 900px the .95fr track
        is ~400px, which is about where a 560px photo stops earning its width and
        the 52ch paragraph starts breaking badly. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .villa-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: clamp(28px, 4.4vw, 44px);
    }
    /* Full-bleed at one column, 560px would be most of a laptop viewport. */
    .villa-plate { height: clamp(300px, 52vw, 480px); }
  }
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .villa-specs { gap: 0 24px; }
  }
  /* ── 520: nothing may push the page sideways. The ledger goes to a single
        column (two label/value rows in ~150px each would break every value onto
        its own line), and both cells already wrap anywhere, so a long unbroken
        string — a URL, a plate number, a hyphenless spec — wraps instead of
        widening the document. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .villa-specs { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); margin-top: 24px; }
    .villa-plate { height: clamp(240px, 62vw, 340px); }
  }
</style>
